\begin{document}

\title{Immutable-CPI: Linear-Time $(1,s)$-Nucleus Decomposition at Scale}

\author{Anonymous Authors}
\affiliation{%
  \institution{Submission \#XXX}
  \country{}
}

\begin{abstract}
The $(r,s)$-nucleus decomposition of a graph ranks each $r$-clique by the number of $s$-cliques ($s > r$) that contain it, producing a hierarchical ranking of densely-connected substructures. The case $r{=}1$ --- the $(1,s)$-vertex nucleus decomposition --- is the most widely applicable variant: it subsumes $k$-core at $s{=}2$, it scales to graphs with millions of vertices, and we show empirically that it matches higher-$r$ variants on community and dense-subgraph discovery tasks at a fraction of their cost.

The state-of-the-art algorithm represents the $s$-clique search space as a mutable \emph{Clique Path Index} (\cpi) and peels the graph by repeatedly updating the index. Its $O(\Sig \log n)$ peeling time and multi-gigabyte memory footprint place large-$s$ regimes and graphs beyond $10^6$ vertices out of reach.

We prove that for $r{=}1$ the \cpi{} can be kept \emph{immutable} throughout peeling: every support update reduces to integer arithmetic on three counters per path. This observation yields an $O(\Sig)$ peeling algorithm and an analytical $8.8\times$ reduction in the dominant per-incidence memory term, and unlocks a construction-time optimization --- the \emph{augmented \sdct} --- that builds all indices in a single pass without ever materializing the \cpi{} tree. A $762$-configuration experimental study on ten large graphs (up to $3$M vertices and $117$M edges, $s$ up to $430$) reports a geometric-mean $7.42\times$ and a maximum $39.4\times$ end-to-end speedup over the state of the art, with peak resident memory reduced by up to $3.25\times$. Four ground-truth case studies on SNAP \texttt{com-dblp} and \texttt{com-youtube} further confirm that $(1,s)$-cores match $(2,s)$ and $(3,s)$ variants on every quality metric we measure while costing $10\times$ to $5{,}000\times$ less.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

