\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Graphs are fundamental data structures for relational information, and their decomposition into hierarchically cohesive substructures is a basic primitive in graph analytics~\cite{nucleusSariyuce14,sigmodCPI}. Classical decomposition models such as the $k$-core~\cite{kcoreSeidman,coreAlg} and $k$-truss~\cite{ktrussCohen,ktrussWang} expose successive layers of dense connectivity by iteratively peeling away low-support vertices or edges; both have been used in community detection~\cite{communityFinder}, anomaly identification, network visualization, and densest-subgraph discovery. To capture higher-order cohesiveness uniformly, Sar{\i}y{\"u}ce et~al.~\cite{nucleusSariyuce14} introduced the \textit{$(r,s)$-nucleus decomposition}: for integers $0 < r < s$, the \textit{$s$-support} of an $r$-clique is the number of distinct $s$-cliques containing it, and the $(r,s)$-core of level $k$ is the maximal subgraph in which every $r$-clique has $s$-support at least $k$. Setting $(r,s){=}(1,2)$ recovers the $k$-core, $(2,3)$ recovers the $k$-truss, and larger parameters produce progressively denser, clique-based hierarchies at higher computational cost.

This paper focuses on the case $r{=}1$, the \textit{$(1,s)$-vertex nucleus decomposition}, in which each vertex $v$ receives a core value $\cores(v)$ equal to the largest $k$ for which $v$ belongs to a subgraph whose every vertex participates in at least $k$ distinct $s$-cliques. The parameter $s$ acts as a granularity knob: at $s{=}2$ the decomposition reduces to $k$-core, at $s{=}3$ it counts triangles per vertex, and at larger $s$ it isolates progressively tighter clique-based kernels.

\stitle{Applications.}
Two empirical observations make $r{=}1$ the operating point most applications use. First, among the parameter combinations $\{(1,s),(2,s),(3,s)\}$ we evaluate in \S\ref{sec:casestudy}, $(1,s)$ matches the others on every quality metric considered while costing $10$ to $5{,}000$ times less. Second, $(1,s)$-decomposition scales to graphs with millions of vertices and to $s$ in the hundreds, whereas $(2,s)$ and $(3,s)$ do not. We illustrate three concrete settings.

\sstitle{Community discovery in collaboration networks.} On scientific co-authorship graphs, $(1,s)$-decomposition with moderate $s$ produces vertex rankings whose top prefix is enriched in tightly knit research groups~\cite{communityFinder,xia2017measuring}. On SNAP's \texttt{com-dblp} at $s{=}10$, the $(1,10)$-core's top-$10{,}000$ recovers $114$ ground-truth communities at the $50$\% threshold and reaches a per-vertex triangle density of $113$ --- numerically identical to what the $(3,10)$-core achieves on the same workload, yet the $(1,10)$ run completes in $13.4$ ms against the $(3,10)$ run's $2{,}380$ ms ($178\times$ slower; \S\ref{sec:casestudy}).

\sstitle{Coordinated-behavior detection.} On interaction or transaction graphs, large-$s$ nuclei isolate small groups whose members repeatedly co-occur in larger cliques, filtering out incidental triangles~\cite{beutel2013copycatch,hooi2016fraudar}. The granularity knob is exactly the analyst's lever: small $s$ admits broad communities, large $s$ exposes coordinated cliques.

\sstitle{Sparse-region analysis on web graphs.} On hyperlink graphs, $(1,s)$ is the only practical $(r,s)$-decomposition at $s$ above a few dozen: $(2,s)$ and $(3,s)$ require explicit $r$-clique enumeration that quickly becomes infeasible. On \texttt{web-it-2004} we run $(1,s)$ for $s$ up to $430$ and recover meaningful sub-structures throughout the range.

\stitle{Existing Methods and Key Limitation.}
The state of the art is the \cpi-based decomposition algorithm of~\cite{sigmodCPI}, which represents the $s$-clique search space as a \textit{Clique Path Index} (\cpi). Each \cpi{} leaf is a pair $(\Vh,\Vp)$ of disjoint vertex sets such that $\Vh\cup\Vp$ is a clique in $G$; the leaf encodes $\binom{|\Vp|}{s-|\Vh|}$ distinct $s$-cliques, namely all sets $\Vh\cup P'$ with $P'\subseteq\Vp$ and $|P'|{=}s-|\Vh|$. Figure~\ref{fig:overview} shows a $10$-vertex running example whose \cpi{} at $s{=}3$ has six leaves encoding ten triangles. The total vertex--leaf incidence count $\Sig=\sum_P(|\holdN{P}|+|\pivN{P}|)$ is the relevant size measure: for graphs with $10^6$ vertices $\Sig$ routinely reaches $10^8$ to $10^9$, yet the \cpi{} never enumerates individual $s$-cliques.

The \cpi-based decomposition is general: a single algorithm covers $r{\in}\{1,2,3,\ldots\}$. Its peel loop, however, treats the \cpi{} as a fully \textit{mutable} index because $r{\ge}2$ peeling can structurally alter leaves. Each peeled $r$-clique triggers, on every leaf it touches, a hash-set membership update, a physical pivot removal from $\Vp$, and a $\Theta(\log n)$ decrease-key in a binary heap for every affected surviving vertex. The vertex-to-leaf hash index alone consumes around $80$ bytes per incidence; at $\Sig{=}10^8$ this is over $7.5$ GiB before any other data structure is allocated. Peeling time is $O(\Sig\log n)$. As a concrete instance, on the largest graph in our study --- \texttt{com-orkut} ($|V|{=}3.1$M, $|E|{=}117$M) --- the SOTA's working set already reaches $200$ GB at $s{=}4$ and exceeds the $503$ GB physical memory of our test machine at $s{=}5$, well below the practically interesting range; our pipeline completes the same $s{=}4$ configuration in $200$ s with $143$ GB of working set (\S\ref{sec:exp-bd}). The structural mutability that drives this overhead is unnecessary for $r{=}1$ in the first place.

\stitle{Research Question.}
This leads to the central question of the paper: \textit{can $(1,s)$-nucleus decomposition be computed without paying the structural-mutability cost that $r{\ge}2$ requires of the state of the art?}

\stitle{Challenges.}
A naive ``just remove the mutability'' approach hits three obstacles inherited from the existing framework.

\sstitle{Per-leaf hash overhead.} Existing peel loops query and modify a per-leaf vertex hash and a reverse vertex-to-leaf multimap. The hash representation costs $\approx 80$ bytes per incidence and is the single largest term in the working set. Eliminating it requires a layout that supports the same access pattern statically.

\sstitle{Heap log factor on every update.} Each peeled vertex changes the support of every surviving incident vertex on every alive leaf. The state-of-the-art binary heap pays $\Theta(\log n)$ per change. The total update count $U$ scales with $\Sig$, so the heap factor is $\Theta(\Sig\log n)$ across the peel.

\sstitle{Tree materialization at construction.} The \sdct{} enumeration that produces the \cpi{} stores its recursion tree to support post-walk traversal. The tree itself consumes $\sim 8\Sig$ bytes plus per-node overhead, even though peeling never queries the parent--child structure.

\stitle{Our Idea.}
We observe that for $r{=}1$ the \cpi{} can be kept fully \textit{immutable} during peeling. When a vertex $v$ is peeled from a leaf $P$ with current pivot count $p_P$ and target size $\eta_P{=}s-|\holdN{P}|$, every surviving incident vertex's support changes by one of two closed-form binomial differences,
\[
  \Delta_{\mathrm{hold}}=\tbinom{p_P}{\eta_P}-\tbinom{p_P-d}{\eta_P},\quad
  \Delta_{\mathrm{pivot}}=\tbinom{p_P-1}{\eta_P-1}-\tbinom{p_P-d-1}{\eta_P-1},
\]
depending on the vertex's role in $P$ and the number $d$ of pivots removed in the batch. The state of every leaf reduces to three integers --- $p_P$, $\eta_P$, and a liveness flag --- with no edits to the vertex sets and no allocations. This invariant, formalised as Theorem~\ref{thm:immutable} and Lemma~\ref{lem:delta} in \S\ref{sec:immutable}, dissolves all three challenges and unlocks two distinct algorithms together with a tree-free construction.

\sstitle{Local-iterative algorithm.} The H-index update of Montresor et~al.~\cite{kcoreLocal} for $k$-core extends to $(1,s)$ via the immutability invariant: each vertex independently recomputes its core value from its leaf-mates' values, with the per-leaf binomial structure of the \cpi{} making the update closed-form. The per-vertex update is independent within an iteration, parallelises trivially across cores, partitions cleanly across machines, and admits an external-memory implementation in which each iteration is one streaming pass over the leaf list (\S\ref{sec:local}).

\sstitle{Centralized peel.} The same invariant collapses the per-leaf hash sets into a single static CSR. Once the layout is static, the bucket-queue peeling discipline of $k$-core~\cite{coreAlg,bucketQueue} applies directly --- supports are bounded non-negative integers, the cursor is monotone non-decreasing, and only decrement operations occur --- whereas the SOTA's mutable hash representation precludes it. Peeling time drops from $O(\Sig\log n)$ to $O(\Sig)$ on a single thread (\S\ref{sec:peel}).

\sstitle{Tree-free \cpi{} construction.} Because the \cpi{}'s parent--child structure is never queried, the \sdct{} enumeration can route each leaf through a zero-overhead callback at the moment of emission. The leaf-to-vertex CSR, the vertex-to-leaf CSR (\S\ref{sec:csr}), and the initial per-vertex supports are all built inline; the \cpi{} tree is never materialised. The dominant per-incidence memory term drops from $\approx 88$ bytes to $\approx 10$ bytes (\S\ref{sec:treefree}).

\stitle{Contributions.}
The contributions of this paper are as follows.
($i$) We prove that the \cpi{} is invariant under $(1,s)$-peeling and that every support update reduces to closed-form integer arithmetic on three counters per leaf (Theorem~\ref{thm:immutable}, Lemma~\ref{lem:delta} in \S\ref{sec:immutable}). The invariant fails for $r{\ge}2$ and is the structural basis for the rest of the paper.
($ii$) We extend the H-index iteration of Montresor et~al.~\cite{kcoreLocal} from $k$-core to $(1,s)$-cores (\S\ref{sec:local}). The H-index update at level $k$ requires the count of $s$-cliques whose every other vertex has value at least $k$; we show this count has a closed-form binomial expression directly on the \cpi{}'s leaves (Lemma~\ref{lem:leaf-ge-k}), so no clique enumeration is needed. Convergence (Lemma~\ref{lem:hindex-fixedpt}) and complexity (Theorem~\ref{thm:local-cost}) follow. Within each iteration the per-vertex update has no shared state, so Algorithm~\ref{alg:local} admits trivial shared-memory, distributed-memory, and external-memory parallelisations.
($iii$) We observe that, by Theorem~\ref{thm:immutable}, peeling never queries the parent--child structure of the \sdct{} tree; only per-leaf data is read. This permits a callback-driven enumeration (Algorithm~\ref{alg:build}, \S\ref{sec:treefree}) that builds the leaf-to-vertex CSR, the vertex-to-leaf CSR, and the initial per-vertex supports in a single $O(\Sig)$ pass, without ever materialising the tree. The callback pattern itself is standard; what is enabled by the immutability invariant is the omission of the tree, which lowers the dominant per-incidence memory from $\approx 88$ to $\approx 10$ bytes.
($iv$) We instantiate the invariant as a centralised, single-thread $O(\Sig)$ peel (Algorithm~\ref{alg:peel}, \S\ref{sec:peel}). The static CSR built by C3 collapses the SOTA's hash-set incidence index; once the layout is static the standard $k$-core bucket-queue peel of~\cite{coreAlg,bucketQueue} applies directly, replacing the SOTA's $O(\Sig \log n)$ heap loop. The static representation is what is enabled by Theorem~\ref{thm:immutable}; the bucket queue itself is classical.
($v$) We report a $762$-configuration wall-clock study on ten large graphs (geometric-mean $7.42\times$, maximum $39.4\times$ speedup over the SOTA baseline; peak memory reduced up to $3.25\times$) and four ground-truth case studies on SNAP's annotated communities (\S\ref{sec:exp}, \S\ref{sec:casestudy}). The case $r{=}1$ matches the quality of $(2,s)$ and $(3,s)$ on every metric we evaluate while costing $10\times$ to $5{,}000\times$ less.

