\stitle{Methodology.}
For every $(graph, s)$ pair in Table~\ref{tab:datasets} we run both implementations on the same input and record three quantities: end-to-end wall-clock time (graph load + degeneracy ordering + \cpi{} construction + peeling + core-value emission), peak resident-set memory via \texttt{getrusage}'s \texttt{ru\_maxrss}, and bit-exact agreement of $\cores(v)$ between the two implementations on every vertex.  All $762$ configurations agree on every output; correctness is therefore not in question.

\stitle{Reproducibility.}
Source code, raw experimental CSVs, and a paper-element-to-script reproduction harness (\texttt{REPRODUCE.md}, \texttt{scripts/verify\_local.sh}, \texttt{scripts/run\_server\_experiments.sh}) are published at \vldbavailabilityurl{}.  Every figure and table in this section can be regenerated from the listed scripts; the local case-study reproductions (\S\ref{sec:casestudy}) finish in under ten minutes on a laptop, the full server reproductions in hours on the hardware listed above.

\subsection{End-to-end speedup and memory}
\label{sec:exp-speed}

Figure~\ref{fig:exp-time} plots wall-clock time as a function of $s$ for each graph, with curves for the centralised peel (Ours) and the SOTA baseline of~\cite{sigmodCPI} (SOTA). Across the parameter range we evaluate, our peel is uniformly at or below the SOTA curve on every graph; the gap between curves widens with $s$ in line with the $O(\Sig)$ versus $O(\Sig \log n)$ analysis of Theorem~\ref{thm:complexity}. Concretely, the peel-loop component accelerates from a $\sim 1.4\times$ ratio at $s{=}3$ to between $14\times$ and $22\times$ at the largest $s$ each graph admits (Table~\ref{tab:bd-time}); end-to-end wall-clock follows the same trend, attenuated by the shared \sdct{} enumeration cost.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_exp_time.pdf}
\caption{End-to-end wall-clock time (ms) as a function of $s$ on every benchmark graph. Solid blue: our centralised peel (\S\ref{sec:peel}); dashed red: the \cpi-based SOTA baseline of~\cite{sigmodCPI}. Both axes are log-scale. The two curves agree at small $s$ where the shared \sdct{} cost dominates and diverge as $s$ grows and peel cost becomes prominent.}
\label{fig:exp-time}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_exp_mem.pdf}
\caption{Peak resident-set memory (MB) as a function of $s$ on every benchmark graph. Solid blue: our pipeline; dashed red: the SOTA. Our footprint is consistently smaller. The gap is largest on graphs whose vertex--leaf incidence count $\Sig$ dominates the working set (e.g.\ \texttt{com-youtube}), where the measured ratio approaches the $8.8\times$ analytical bound of Table~\ref{tab:mem}; on graphs where the input adjacency dominates RSS, the analytical \cpi{}-side savings are obscured by constant overhead.}
\label{fig:exp-mem}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:exp-mem} reports peak resident-set memory under the same per-graph layout. Our pipeline's RSS curve sits below the SOTA's on every graph and at every $s$, with the largest gap on graphs whose working set is dominated by the \cpi{} index (e.g.\ \texttt{com-youtube}, where the ratio narrows toward the $8.8\times$ analytical bound of Table~\ref{tab:mem} but tops out at $3.33\times$ at the largest $s$ tested --- the residual gap reflects the $4n$ support array and the input-adjacency term, both of which are independent of $\Sig$). On graphs whose RSS is dominated by the input adjacency itself --- such as the high-edge-count \texttt{web-it-2004} --- the gap is narrower, reflecting the constant-overhead terms that the \cpi{} redesign does not affect.

\subsection{Local-iterative vs.\ centralised peel}
\label{sec:exp-local}

The local-iterative algorithm of \S\ref{sec:local} produces output bit-exact identical to the centralised peel on every configuration, confirming the equivalence of Lemma~\ref{lem:hindex-fixedpt}'s fixed point with the peeling sequence. On a single thread the centralised peel is faster by the factor $T$ predicted by Theorem~\ref{thm:local-cost}: across the configurations we measure, $T$ ranges from a few dozen H-index iterations at small $s$ to a few thousand at large $s$, and the per-iteration $O(\Sig \log d_{\max})$ work is dominated in absolute terms by the peel's $O(\Sig)$ single pass. The local algorithm is not presented as a single-thread improvement; its value is in the parallel, distributed, and external-memory regimes that the centralised peel cannot address (\S\ref{sec:conclusion}). A full empirical comparison of $T$ as a function of $s$ and a parallel-scaling study are deferred to the final version.

\subsection{Time and memory breakdown by phase}
\label{sec:exp-breakdown}

To localise the source of the speedup and the memory reduction, we instrument every phase boundary in the pipeline and re-run a $17$-configuration subset with per-phase logging: \texttt{com-youtube} at $s\in\{3,5,8,12,16\}$, \texttt{web-Stanford} at $s\in\{3,5,10,20,40,60\}$, and \texttt{web-it-2004} at $s\in\{3,10,30,100,200,400\}$, three runs each, $102$ runs in total.

The pipeline has three coarse phases: \textit{load} (graph parse plus degeneracy order), \textit{build} (the \sdct{} enumeration that both algorithms share, plus, for the SOTA, tree storage and hash-set construction), and \textit{peel} (Algorithm~\ref{alg:peel} for ours, the heap-based loop for the SOTA). Table~\ref{tab:bd-time} reports the per-phase wall-clock medians. The build phase dominates total time at every $(graph, s)$: between $60$\% and $99$\% of the total wall-clock is spent walking the \sdct{}. Both algorithms share the enumeration backbone of \S\ref{sec:sdct}, so their build times agree to within a few percent. The wall-clock advantage of our pipeline therefore comes almost entirely from peel, where it grows from $1.4\times$ at $s{=}3$ to $22\times$ at $s{=}60$ on \texttt{web-Stanford}, and from $1.3\times$ at $s{=}3$ to $14\times$ at $s{=}400$ on \texttt{web-it-2004}. The peel speedup grows monotonically with $s$ on every graph: as $s$ increases, the number of broadcast updates per leaf grows, and the $O(1)$ bucket relocation per update progressively widens the gap against the heap's $\Theta(\log n)$ decrease-key, in line with Theorem~\ref{thm:complexity}.

