Table~\ref{tab:bd-mem} reports peak resident-set memory across the same configurations. Both algorithms shrink with $s$ as the number of leaves drops, and our peak is consistently smaller. The reduction ratio grows with $s$, reaching $3.33\times$ on \texttt{com-youtube} at $s{=}16$, where the working set is dominated by the \cpi{} index. On \texttt{web-it-2004} the ratio stays in the $1.3$--$1.6\times$ band because the $7.2$M-edge graph adjacency itself accounts for $\sim 200$ MB and dampens the index-side savings. This pattern is consistent with the analytical $88\Sig \to 10\Sig$ reduction of Table~\ref{tab:mem}: as $\Sig$ comes to dominate the working set, the measured ratio moves toward the analytical $8.8\times$ bound (Tab.~\ref{tab:bd-mem}'s peak of $3.33\times$ at $s{=}16$ is $\sim 38\%$ of the bound; the residual gap is the $\Sig$-independent overhead).

