\stitle{Implications.}
The breakdown identifies two regimes. In the \emph{build-bound} regime (large $\Sig$, modest $s$), build dominates total wall-clock and our advantage is the per-incidence memory reduction; total-time speedup is in the $1.0$--$1.3\times$ range. In the \emph{peel-bound} regime (large $s$ relative to $\Sig$), build collapses to a fraction of total wall-clock and the $O(\Sig)$ vs.\ $O(\Sig \log n)$ peel advantage produces the $14$--$39\times$ headline speedups of \S\ref{sec:exp-speed}. The $762$-configuration aggregate ($7.42\times$ geometric mean) is a weighted average over both regimes; readers planning a deployment should use Table~\ref{tab:bd-time} to estimate the speedup expected on their workload.

\subsection{Per-graph breakdown}
\label{sec:exp-bd}

Table~\ref{tab:per-graph} summarises the $762$-configuration results separated by graph. The geometric-mean speedup ranges from $2.45\times$ on \texttt{com-orkut} (where only $s \in [2,4]$ fits in our memory budget) to $19.25\times$ on \texttt{com-dblp} (where the workload is dominated by the peel and the bucket queue therefore yields a near-pure $\Theta(\log n)$ saving). \texttt{web-Stanford} reports the highest single-configuration speedup ($39.4\times$ at $s{=}61$), driven by short absolute timings. \texttt{web-it-2004} exercises the large-$s$ regime ($s$ up to $430$): even at $s{=}430$, where each implementation completes in milliseconds, ours maintains a $19.3\times$ wall-clock advantage. The overall geometric mean of $7.42\times$ and the maximum of $39.4\times$ are both consistent with the $O(\Sig \log n) \to O(\Sig)$ analysis of Theorem~\ref{thm:complexity}.

