\end{table}

\sstitle{$(1,3)$-core already beats $k$-core.}
Even at the lowest non-trivial $s$, $(1,3)$-core lifts precision from $0.504$ to $0.523$ ($+3.8$\%) and the number of $\ge 50\%$-recovered communities from $94$ to $114$ ($+21$\%). All this with no compute budget difference --- $(1,3)$-decomposition is comparable in cost to $k$-core in our pipeline.

\sstitle{Quality saturates by $s{=}5$.}
$s \in \{3, 5, 10\}$ all produce identical $K{=}10{,}000$ rankings: $0.523$ precision, $114$ recovered communities, $113.4$ tri/v.  The right ranking structure is captured at the lowest non-trivial $s$ and stays stable; bumping $s$ inside this regime is free quality with shrinking working set.

\sstitle{Larger $s$ isolates denser substructures.}
Once the active set itself drops below $K$, the picture changes.  $(1,15)$-core's top-$6{,}767$ subgraph has $\frac{154}{112} \approx 1.4\times$ the triangle density of $k$-core's top-$10{,}000$, with precision $0.548$ (the highest in the column).  Pushing further, $(1,30)$-core's top-$1{,}358$ subgraph reaches $523$ triangles per vertex --- $4.7\times$ denser than $k$-core's top-$10{,}000$ at $\frac{1}{7}$ the vertex count.

\sstitle{Practical guideline.}
For ground-truth-style community recovery on \texttt{com-dblp}, $s{=}5$ is a no-regret choice: same precision and rec50 as $k$-core's-strict-superset $(1,3)$, smaller active set.  Push $s$ higher only when the analytic question demands a near-clique core (e.g., conference-cohort discovery).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/cs3_precision.pdf}
\caption{Precision@$K$ on \texttt{com-dblp} (SNAP top-$5000$ community ground truth). $(1,s)$-cores for $s \in \{3, 5, 10, 15\}$ all surpass $k$-core across the entire range of $K$; gains widen as $K$ shrinks, indicating that the absolute top of the ranking is more selectively in-community than $k$-core's.}
\label{fig:cs3}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Case study 3: $(1,s)$ is Pareto-optimal among $(r,s)$}
\label{sec:cs-pareto}

We now ask whether higher $r$ buys additional quality. Fixing $s{=}10$ on \texttt{com-dblp} and varying $r \in \{1, 2, 3\}$ produces Table~\ref{tab:cs4}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-$r$ comparison at $s{=}10$ on \texttt{com-dblp}. ``Time'' is end-to-end wall-clock for the decomposition step (our implementation for $r{=}1$, the same SOTA codebase~\cite{sigmodCPI} for $r \ge 2$). $(1,10)$-core matches $(3,10)$-core on every quality metric while running $178\times$ faster.}
\label{tab:cs4}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Method            & Precision & rec50 & tri/v & Time (ms) \\
\midrule
$k$-core          & $0.504$   & $94$   & $112.3$ & $\phantom{0\,}2.3$ \\
$(1,10)$-core     & $0.523$   & $114$  & $113.4$ & $13.4$ \\
$(2,10)$-core     & $0.529$   & $113$  & $\phantom{0}71.8$  & $123.1$\\
$(3,10)$-core     & $0.524$   & $114$  & $113.4$ & $2{,}379.6$\\
\midrule
\textbf{(1) vs.\ (3)} & $-0.001$ & $0$   & $0.0$ & $-178\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\sstitle{$(1,10)$ and $(3,10)$ are quality-equivalent.}
The two cores produce \emph{identical} community-recovery counts ($114$ and $114$), \emph{identical} triangle densities ($113.4$ each), and precision differences within sampling noise ($0.523$ vs.\ $0.524$).  The two rankings disagree on at most $1$ vertex per $1{,}000$ at every $K$ we tested.

\sstitle{$(2,10)$ \emph{distorts} triangle density.}
$(2,10)$-core gains $1.1$\% in precision but recovers \emph{fewer} communities ($113$ vs.\ $114$) and gives $40$\% \emph{less} triangle density ($71.8$ vs.\ $113.4$).  The edge-to-vertex projection step (every vertex inherits the maximum core of its incident edges) discards inter-edge structure, biasing the ranking towards bridge endpoints.

\sstitle{Cost penalty grows quickly with $r$.}
$(1,10)$ runs in $13.4$ ms, $(2,10)$ in $123$ ms ($9.2\times$ slower), and $(3,10)$ in $2{,}380$ ms ($178\times$ slower).  At the same quality, the cost penalty is $9{-}178\times$.  The constant grows in $r$ because the number of $r$-cliques per $s$-clique is $\binom{s}{r}$ (so at $s{=}10$, going from $r{=}1$ to $r{=}3$ multiplies the tracked incidences from $\binom{10}{1}{=}10$ to $\binom{10}{3}{=}120$, a $12\times$ inflation before the mutable-tree maintenance overhead kicks in).

\sstitle{Across the $(r,s)$ grid.}
The pattern from Table~\ref{tab:cs4} is not specific to $s{=}10$.  Figure~\ref{fig:cs6} plots the Pareto frontier of (precision, runtime) over an $r{\times}s$ grid, $r \in \{1,2,3\}$, $s \in \{5,10,15,20\}$, on \texttt{com-dblp}.  The $r{=}1$ frontier dominates the entire $r \in \{2, 3\}$ region: at every quality level achieved by $(2,s)$ or $(3,s)$, some $(1,s')$ achieves it faster, often by orders of magnitude.

\sstitle{Practical guideline.}
For community-recovery on \texttt{com-dblp}, increasing $r$ above $1$ is pure cost.  Tune $s$ to set the granularity (CS-1) and keep $r{=}1$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/cs6_pareto.pdf}
\caption{Pareto plot of community-recovery quality vs.\ runtime across the $(r,s)$ grid on \texttt{com-dblp}, $r \in \{1,2,3\}$, $s \in \{5, 10, 15, 20\}$. The $r{=}1$ frontier (blue) dominates the $r{\ge}2$ region (green, red) at every operating point; $(1,s)$ and $(3,s)$ cluster on the same quality contour, separated horizontally by $10\times$ to $5{,}000\times$ in runtime.}
\label{fig:cs6}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Case study 4: cross-domain validation on \texttt{com-youtube}}
\label{sec:cs-youtube}

To check that the Pareto picture is not specific to academic collaboration, we replay the cross-$r$ comparison on \texttt{com-youtube} (Table~\ref{tab:cs7}, Figure~\ref{fig:cs7y}).

