\sstitle{Pattern is identical to \texttt{com-dblp}.}
At every $s$, $(1,s)$-core matches the quality ceiling of $(3,s)$-core within sampling noise (e.g., $\Delta\,$rec50 $\le 5$ at $s{=}5$, exact match at $s{\ge}10$).

\sstitle{$s{=}10$: rankings are bit-exact identical.}
At $(s,r)\in\{(10,1),(10,2),(10,3)\}$, the rec50 and tri/v columns of Table~\ref{tab:cs7} are pairwise identical ($49$ / $293$ for all three): $r{=}1$, $r{=}2$, and $r{=}3$ produce the \emph{same ranking} at $s{=}10$ on \texttt{com-youtube}.  The cost difference is gross: $31$ ms ($r{=}1$) vs.\ $2{,}093$ ms ($r{=}3$) --- a $67.5\times$ gap.

\sstitle{$(2,5)$ trades quality for cost.}
On the social-network domain $(2,s)$ shows the same triangle-density distortion as on collaboration: $161$ vs.\ $173$ tri/v at $s{=}5$ and $293$ vs.\ $293$ at $s{=}10$ (matched there only because the active set has shrunk past the $K{=}10\,000$ point).  $(2,s)$ is never strictly better than $(1,s)$.

\sstitle{Practical guideline.}
The cross-domain take is identical to CS-3: pick $r{=}1$ and tune $s$.  $(1,15)$ at $9$ ms produces the same active-set ($95$ vertices) as $(3,15)$ at $87$ ms but $9.8\times$ faster --- and on \texttt{com-youtube} that active set is below the smallest SNAP community, so $r$ has no leverage anyway.

