\subsection{Case study 5: ego-network of an arbitrary anchor}
\label{sec:cs-viz}

We visualize the $2$-hop ego-network of a single \texttt{com-dblp} anchor (vertex $52213$, $|\mathcal{E}|{=}474$) under three settings of $s$ (Figure~\ref{fig:cs9}, summary metrics in Table~\ref{tab:cs9}). The same neighbourhood is alternately an opaque community ($s$ small) or a precise lattice of research teams ($s$ large).

\sstitle{$s{=}3$: undifferentiated blob.}
$(1,3)$-core preserves $462$ of the $474$ vertices in a single connected component (intra-frac $0.992$, avg conductance $0.004$).  At this granularity the community structure is invisible: every vertex is reachable from every other through residual edges.

\sstitle{$s{=}5$: dominant cluster + satellites.}
$(1,5)$-core retains $335$ vertices but already partitions into $5$ components, with the largest holding $329$ ($98.2$\% of survivors).  Intra-frac drops to $0.796$ and conductance rises to $0.121$ --- the blob has cracked but not fractured.

\sstitle{$s{=}10$: precise lattice of teams.}
$(1,10)$-core collapses to $66$ vertices in $26$ tightly-bonded modules (largest CC has $32$ members).  Intra-frac is $0.340$, avg conductance $0.636$: the survivors form well-separated micro-modules with negligible cross-module connectivity.

\sstitle{Conductance jumps $160\times$.}
Across the three settings, average component conductance rises from $0.004$ to $0.121$ to $0.636$ --- two-and-a-half orders of magnitude --- while $|V|$ shrinks $7\times$.  The trade-off is monotone: more $s$ buys more separation per surviving vertex.

\sstitle{Practical guideline.}
For ego-network exploration, $s{=}10$ is a generic ``zoom-in'' setting on academic-collaboration graphs: it surfaces tight collaboration teams without producing isolated singletons.  Smaller $s$ is appropriate only when the analyst wants the broad neighbourhood, not its modular decomposition.

