\label{tab:cs9}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{6pt}
\begin{tabular}{r r r r r r}
\toprule
$s$ & $|V|$ & CCs & largest CC & intra-frac & avg cond \\
\midrule
$3$  & $462$ & $\phantom{0}1$ & $462$ & $0.992$ & $0.004$ \\
$5$  & $335$ & $\phantom{0}5$ & $329$ & $0.796$ & $0.121$ \\
$10$ & $\phantom{0}66$  & $26$ & $\phantom{0}32$  & $0.340$ & $0.636$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/cs9_egonet.pdf}
\caption{$2$-hop ego-network of a \texttt{com-dblp} anchor under $(1,s)$-decomposition, $s \in \{3, 5, 10\}$. Vertices are colored by their connected component in the induced subgraph of $(1,s)$-survivors. At $s{=}3$ (left), $462$ vertices form one tangled blob; at $s{=}10$ (right), $66$ vertices separate into $26$ tight micro-modules. Stronger $s$ exposes finer modular structure.}
\label{fig:cs9}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Case study 6: the $(1,s)$-nucleus hierarchy across domains}
\label{sec:cs-hier}

The $(1,s)$-cores form a nested family: at any threshold $k$ the surviving vertex set $\{v : \cores(v) \ge k\}$ induces a subgraph whose components are subsets of those at any lower $k$.  We summarise this family with the textbook elder-rule join tree~\cite{morozov2007persistence} (standard $0$-dim persistent homology; no algorithmic novelty in the elder rule itself --- our novelty is its peel-integrated realisation of Theorem~\ref{thm:peel-hier}): each branch is a component, born at the $k$ where it first reaches $\ge 5$ vertices and dying at the $k$ where it merges into an older sibling.  Each branch carries a persistence pair $(k_\mathrm{birth}, k_\mathrm{death})$.  We run it on three graphs from distinct domains: \texttt{com-dblp} (collaboration, $n{=}317$k, $m{=}1.05$M), \texttt{com-youtube} (social/video, $n{=}1.13$M, $m{=}2.99$M), \texttt{web-Stanford} (web hyperlinks, $n{=}282$k, $m{=}1.99$M).  Table~\ref{tab:cs10cross} consolidates the headline counts; Figures~\ref{fig:cs10}--\ref{fig:cs10p} render the trees and persistence diagrams.

\sstitle{Wall-clock cost is negligible.}
Per-graph build time ranges from sub-millisecond (\texttt{com-youtube} at $s{=}15$, only $95$ active vertices) to $363$ ms (\texttt{web-Stanford} at $s{=}3$), single-threaded Python, median over $5$ runs (\texttt{bench\_multigraph.py}; all $12$ configurations $< 1$ s).  This validates Theorem~\ref{thm:peel-hier} empirically: hierarchy extraction is essentially free relative to peeling.  Correctness checked against a hand-traced 12-vertex brute force and an independent snapshot-per-threshold reference (Phase A of \texttt{bench\_hierarchy.py}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Branches / internal absorption events / max tree depth produced by the elder-rule join tree on three domains, $s \in \{3, 5, 10, 15\}$.  Three distinct profiles appear: \texttt{com-youtube}'s tree collapses by $s{=}5$, \texttt{com-dblp} retains structure until $s{\approx}10$, and \texttt{web-Stanford} preserves a multi-level hierarchy across the entire range.}
\label{tab:cs10cross}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{l rrr rrr rrr rrr}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{$s{=}3$} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{$s{=}5$} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{$s{=}10$} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{$s{=}15$} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\cmidrule(lr){8-10}\cmidrule(lr){11-13}
graph & br. & int. & d & br. & int. & d & br. & int. & d & br. & int. & d \\
\midrule
\texttt{com-dblp}     & 9{,}968 & 1{,}175 & 8 & 5{,}794 & 583   & 7 &   664 &   8 & 1 & 222 &   2 & 1 \\
\texttt{com-youtube}  & 3{,}568 &      61 & 2 &     280 &   1   & 1 &    14 &   0 & 0 &  -- &  -- & -- \\
\texttt{web-Stanford} & 8{,}731 & 1{,}776 & 7 & 6{,}473 & 1{,}032 & 8 & 1{,}946 & 277 & 3 & 1{,}573 & 220 & 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\sstitle{com-dblp (collaboration) collapses moderately fast.}  Tree depth $8 \to 7 \to 1 \to 1$ as $s$ grows: rich nested communities at small $s$, near-flat partition by $s{=}10$.  This matches the granularity story of \S\ref{sec:cs-granularity} --- at large $s$ the graph fragments into many tightly-knit research groups that never share a larger super-core.

\sstitle{com-youtube (social) collapses immediately.}  Even at $s{=}3$ the tree depth is only $2$, and by $s{=}15$ no $5$-vertex component survives at all (the $95$ active vertices form smaller components only).  The friendship graph has fewer triangles per vertex than the academic collaboration graph, so the clique-driven nesting that drives the $(1,s)$-hierarchy is largely absent.

\sstitle{web-Stanford preserves hierarchy across the entire range.}  At every $s$ tested the tree retains $\ge 1{,}500$ branches with non-trivial depth ($7, 8, 3, 2$), and even at $s{=}15$ there are $220$ internal absorption events.  The web hyperlink graph contains densely interlinked clusters at every clique scale, so the $(1,s)$-decomposition exposes a multi-level hierarchy that is largely robust to $s$.  This is qualitatively different from both social-network behaviours.

\sstitle{Practical guideline.}
The collapse rate of the join tree (Tab.~\ref{tab:cs10cross}'s ``int.'' column) is a domain fingerprint.  For graphs whose tree empties out by $s{=}5$ (e.g., social networks), small $s$ already saturates the granularity knob.  For graphs whose tree survives high $s$ (e.g., web), the analyst gains analytical resolution by pushing $s$ further.

