\label{fig:cs10}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/cs10_persistence.pdf}
\caption{Persistence diagrams across three domains (rows) and four values of $s$ (cols).  Each point is one branch at $(k_\mathrm{death}{+}1, k_\mathrm{birth}{+}1)$; marker area $\propto \sqrt{|C|}$ at death.  The three domains exhibit distinctly different cloud envelopes: \texttt{com-dblp} (top) collapses to a left edge by $s{=}10$, \texttt{com-youtube} (middle) collapses by $s{=}5$, \texttt{web-Stanford} (bottom) preserves a triangular cloud (= rich cross-$k$ structure) across the entire range.}
\label{fig:cs10p}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Case study 7: clique-coherent vs edge-based hierarchy}
\label{sec:cs-clique}

The hierarchy of CS-6 uses plain edge connectivity, the standard choice for join-tree algorithms.  A more $(1,s)$-faithful definition replaces edges with $s$-cliques: vertices $u, v$ are connected at level $k$ iff there exists an $s$-clique $C \subseteq G$ with $u, v \in C$ and $\min_{w \in C} \cores(w) \ge k$.  Two vertices link only when they co-star in some surviving $s$-clique, not merely when they share an edge.

\sstitle{Algorithm: Kruskal on $s$-cliques.}
Enumerate every $s$-clique of $G$ via the augmented \sdct{} of \S\ref{sec:treefree}, sort cliques by $\min_{w \in C} \cores(w)$ descending, and union all $s$ vertices of each clique using the elder-rule bookkeeping of CS-6.  At higher $s$ the explicit clique enumeration is replaced by SDCT leaf streaming (\S\ref{sec:augsdct}); the algorithm is otherwise unchanged.  This variant has no analogue in plain $k$-core / $k$-truss literature: it is genuinely $(1,s)$-specific because it uses the SDCT's compact representation of $s$-cliques as the connectivity primitive.

\sstitle{Clique-coherent surfaces a finer hierarchy.}
On \texttt{com-dblp} at $s{=}3$ ($\sim 1.0$M triangles enumerated in $0.7$ s, hierarchy built in $1.4$ s), the clique-coherent tree has $10{,}427$ branches with $1{,}507$ internal absorption events and depth $10$.  The edge-based variant has only $9{,}968$ branches, $1{,}175$ events and depth $8$ at the same $s$.  The clique-coherent definition is strictly finer (Figure~\ref{fig:cs10b}).

\sstitle{Why: bridge edges are unmasked.}
$459$ branches and $332$ absorption events are added by the clique definition.  The intuition is that vertices which share an edge but never co-star in a triangle no longer merge.  Weakly-bonded bridge edges --- which inflate the active components in the edge-based view --- no longer mask the underlying clique structure.

\sstitle{Practical guideline.}
For \emph{algorithmic} consistency with $(1,s)$-decomposition, prefer the clique-coherent hierarchy.  For raw cost on a single $(s, k)$ filtration, the edge-based hierarchy is cheaper (no $s$-clique enumeration).  Both produce the same persistence diagram envelope; only the densest core of the diagram differs.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/cs10b_compare.pdf}
\caption{Edge-based (left) vs clique-coherent (right) persistence diagrams on \texttt{com-dblp} at $s{=}3$.  Same axes; markers as in Figure~\ref{fig:cs10p}.  Clique-coherent has $459$ more branches and a deeper tree (depth $10$ vs $8$): vertices linked by edges but never by triangles no longer merge.}
\label{fig:cs10b}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Summary of the case studies}
\label{sec:cs-summary}

The four ground-truth case studies and the three qualitative visualizations (ego-network, cross-domain hierarchy, clique-coherent variant) together support the headline claim of this paper: among the parameter combinations $\{(1,s), (2,s), (3,s)\}$, the case $r{=}1$ is the right operating point. It matches the quality of higher-$r$ variants on every metric we evaluate ---  precision, community recovery, triangle density, ego-network visibility, and hierarchy depth --- and the $10\times$--$5{,}000\times$ runtime advantage we measure makes large-$s$ regimes computationally feasible.

%===============================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\stitle{$k$-core and $k$-truss.}
The $k$-core decomposition~\cite{kcoreSeidman,coreAlg} ranks each vertex by the largest $k$ such that the vertex belongs to a subgraph of minimum degree $k$, and is the first member of the $(r,s)$-nucleus family at $(r,s){=}(1,2)$. The Batagelj--Zaver{\v s}nik algorithm~\cite{coreAlg} computes it in $O(m)$ time using bucket-based peeling --- the same primitive we use in \S\ref{sec:bucket}. The $k$-truss decomposition~\cite{ktrussCohen,ktrussWang} corresponds to $(r,s){=}(2,3)$, peeling edges by their incident-triangle count. Both produce \emph{hierarchies} of nested subgraphs and are widely deployed for community discovery, but neither generalizes to higher cliques without explicit clique enumeration. For survey-style coverage of $k$-truss applications see~\cite{ktrussSurvey}.

\stitle{Nucleus decomposition.}
The general $(r,s)$-nucleus framework was introduced by Sar{\i}y{\"u}ce et~al.~\cite{nucleusSariyuce14} and extended in subsequent work~\cite{nucleusSariyuceTODS}, with peeling implementations that explicitly enumerate $s$-cliques and maintain per-$r$-clique support counts. Their reference algorithm runs in $O(\text{\# }s\text{-cliques})$ time per peeling step and is impractical for $s \ge 4$ on graphs with millions of vertices: the number of $s$-cliques grows super-polynomially, making explicit enumeration the binding constraint.

\stitle{The CPI baseline.}
The state-of-the-art~\cite{sigmodCPI} sidesteps explicit enumeration by representing the $s$-clique search space as a Clique Path Index (\cpi), built via a degeneracy-ordered Bron--Kerbosch~\cite{bronkerbosch} recursion with Tomita pivoting~\cite{tomita,eppstein}. Each leaf encodes $\binom{|\Vp|}{s-|\Vh|}$ cliques symbolically. Their peeling loop maintains the \cpi{} as a mutable tree, paying $O(\Sig \log n)$ time and $O(\Sig)$ memory at $\sim 80$ bytes per incidence. Our work focuses on $r{=}1$ and proves that within this special case the \cpi{} is invariant under peeling, removing the dominant cost. We do not change the \cpi{} construction algorithm itself --- the augmented \sdct{} of \S\ref{sec:treefree} reuses the same enumeration order --- but we eliminate the tree representation by routing leaves through a callback at emission time. The general $r \ge 2$ algorithm of~\cite{sigmodCPI} remains the state of the art for those cases; extending the present technique to $r{=}2,3$ is the most promising future direction (\S\ref{sec:conclusion}).

\stitle{Clique enumeration and pivoting.}
Tomita pivoting~\cite{tomita} reduces the recursion fan-out of Bron--Kerbosch by selecting a pivot vertex whose non-neighbors form the only branching set; in degeneracy-ordered recursion this gives $O(d \cdot 3^{d/3})$ enumeration time~\cite{eppstein}, where $d$ is the graph's degeneracy. The Pivoter algorithm~\cite{pivoter} uses the same pivot-set decomposition to count, but not enumerate, $s$-cliques; the \sdct{} of~\cite{sigmodCPI} extends this to recover the encoded leaves needed for nucleus decomposition. Our augmented \sdct{} (\S\ref{sec:augsdct}) is a drop-in replacement at the enumeration interface and inherits the same per-leaf complexity.

\stitle{Bucket-based priority queues.}
The bucket queue we use in \S\ref{sec:bucket} is a special case of the multi-level bucket structures of~\cite{bucketQueue}; in our setting the keys (clique-supports) are non-negative integers bounded by the largest clique count, the cursor is monotone non-decreasing, and only decrease-keys occur, so the simplest single-level array bucket is sufficient. The same primitive is the basis of $k$-core peeling~\cite{coreAlg}; we extend it to $(1,s)$ by combining it with the immutable-\cpi{} delta formulas (Lemma~\ref{lem:delta}).

\stitle{Community discovery as ground truth.}
We evaluate against SNAP's annotated communities~\cite{communityFinder} on \texttt{com-dblp} and \texttt{com-youtube}, following the established practice in the nucleus-decomposition and dense-subgraph literature~\cite{sigmodCPI,nucleusSariyuce14}. The metrics we use --- precision@$K$, communities recovered, and intrinsic triangle density --- are standard for this evaluation pattern.

%===============================================================
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper studied the $(1,s)$-nucleus decomposition problem and presented an algorithmic redesign organised around a single structural property: for $r{=}1$ the Clique Path Index is invariant under peeling (Theorem~\ref{thm:immutable}), and every support update reduces to closed-form integer arithmetic on three counters per leaf (Lemma~\ref{lem:delta}). Two algorithms instantiate the invariant. The local-iterative algorithm (\S\ref{sec:local}) is an H-index fixed-point iteration with no shared queue, no decrease-key, and no global ordering, which serves as the foundation for parallel, distributed, and external-memory deployments. The centralised peel (\S\ref{sec:peel}) is an $O(\Sig)$ single-thread algorithm using a static CSR and a bucket array. A construction-side augmented \sdct{} (\S\ref{sec:treefree}) feeds both algorithms in a single pass without materialising the \cpi{} tree. Across $762$ configurations on ten large graphs, the centralised peel achieves a geometric-mean $7.42\times$ and maximum $39.4\times$ end-to-end speedup over the state of the art, with peak resident memory reduced by up to $3.25\times$. Four ground-truth case studies confirm that $(1,s)$-cores match the quality of $(2,s)$ and $(3,s)$-cores on every metric we evaluate while costing $10\times$ to $5{,}000\times$ less.

\stitle{Future Work.}
Several directions remain open. First, the local-iterative algorithm of \S\ref{sec:local} is intentionally shared-nothing, and its parallel, distributed, and external-memory potential warrants empirical investigation. Concretely, four extensions are immediate. (i)~\textit{Many-core and GPU.} Algorithm~\ref{alg:local} is bulk-synchronous and parallelises trivially across cores or GPU threads; per-iteration scaling should be limited only by memory bandwidth. (ii)~\textit{Distributed shared-nothing.} Partitioning vertices across machines and replicating leaves that span partitions yields a deployment in which one iteration is a single all-to-all gather of changed values followed by local H-index updates~\cite{kcoreLocal}; no central coordinator is required. (iii)~\textit{External-memory.} When $\Sig$ exceeds RAM, each iteration is one streaming pass over the leaf list with the candidate vector $O(n)$ in RAM. (iv)~\textit{Dynamic and streaming graphs.} A vertex insertion restarts H-index updates only at the new vertex and its leaf-mates; the local algorithm therefore supports incremental updates in a way that the peeling-order discipline of the centralised peel does not.

Second, generalising Theorem~\ref{thm:immutable} to $r \ge 2$ would extend the algorithmic gains beyond the vertex case. The proof relies on the fact that vertex removal does not split a leaf's pivot set; for $r{=}2$, edge removal can disconnect $\holdN{P}$ from a subset of remaining pivots, and partial-immutability conditions must be identified before the corresponding savings can be realised. Third, supporting vertex \textit{deletions} on the local algorithm with the same locality guarantees as insertions remains open.

